      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1881, Sitting Bull and his band surrendered to U.S. forces roughly at what is now Plentywood, cementing the town’s place in Native American history.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://discoveringmontana.com/towns/plentywood/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The first business opened in 1900, and a post office followed in 1902, marking the start of Plentywood’s commercial life.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plentywood%2C_Montana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Plentywood became the county seat of Sheridan County, anchoring its role as the region’s administrative hub.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plentywood%2C_Montana",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Great Northern Railway’s branch line reached Plentywood in 1912, linking the town to the broader rail network.",
